Practical No. 4

Introduction to Shell Scripting

Create and execute simple shell scripts.

Theory: A shell script is a text file containing a sequence of commands that the shell interprets and executes in order.
It starts with a shebang (#!/bin/bash) line specifying the path to the interpreter.

Requirement:

•Linux
•WSL Terminal

a) Create a script file using vi or nano.

b) Write a simple script using echo statements:

$ nano name.sh

#Code inside file:

#!/bin/bash
echo "Welcome to linux shell scripting"
echo "Ubuntu platform"

Opens a terminal text editor to build the script.

echo outputs text blocks directly onto the standard terminal stream.

c) Make script executable using chmod + x:

$ chmod +x name.sh

Text files lack execution rights by default.
chmod +x updates permission to let the file run like a binary.

d) Execute script and observe output:

$ ./name.sh

Using ./ triggers the file execution inside the active working directory, 
prompting the shell to read the code.
